# evidence-cmd: cd plugins/foundry/mcp-server && uv run --quiet --with pytest pytest tests/test_liveness.py -v --no-header -p no:cacheprovider
# evidence-for: US-006, FR-015, FR-025, AC-021
# evidence-volatile: \d+\.\d+s
# evidence-volatile: Installed \d+ packages? in \d+(\.\d+)?ms
# evidence-timeout: 300
#
# The READ half of the FR-015 loop. Every name below is an assertion about
# foundry_liveness reading real ledger files off disk; no clock is mocked and
# no filesystem is faked, so a passing name means the tool behaves that way
# against wall-clock time exactly as it will in a run.
#
# The four names to read first:
#   ..._stalled_and_progressing_are_distinct_statuses_in_one_report  AC-021/OT-010
#   ..._the_threshold_boundary_decides_the_status                    the comparison itself
#   ..._fresh_lines_on_an_unchanged_step_report_no_progress          A-025's second case
#   ..._no_ledger_at_all_is_an_empty_roster_not_an_error             CT-004's empty input
#
# FR-025's derivation is pinned by the four threshold_* names: the constant is
# above 5x the shortest measured working batch and below half the median, so
# retuning it to a "generic constant" turns them red.
#
# GRIND cycle 4 added two sections. D-022 — a finished agent stops writing, so
# without a terminal state it ages into `stalled` and silts up needs_attention
# until the list stops being read:
#   ..._a_finished_agent_reports_done_however_long_ago_it_finished  the defect
#   ..._a_finished_agent_does_not_fill_needs_attention              its real cost
#   ..._a_resumed_agent_leaves_done_behind          terminal is the LAST line,
#                                                   not a latch on the file
# D-021 — the four INSPECT stream agents are spawned from the F2 roster, not
# from either tool in this module, so a glob over progress/ had no row for
# them at all and their silence was indistinguishable from success:
#   ..._expected_stream_agents_with_no_ledger_are_reported        the defect
#   ..._a_missing_stream_record_carries_the_block_that_fixes_it   the remedy
#                                                                 travels with it
#   ..._streams_are_not_expected_outside_inspect     and the guards against the
#   ..._a_just_spawned_stream_is_not_yet_late        permanent false alarm that
#   ..._conditional_streams_are_only_expected_...    would rebuild D-022
#
# GRIND cycle 5 added the D-058 section. The roster was built from the ledger
# files that EXIST plus an expected-STREAM set, so a teammate the run had
# dispatched which died before writing line one contributed to neither half:
# it was absent from the report and needs_attention said nothing was wrong.
# The roster now also reads spawns.log, the run's own record of what it
# dispatched. Read these first:
#   ..._a_teammate_that_died_before_line_one_is_on_the_roster   the defect, in
#                                                   the F1 scenario PROVE drove
#   ..._a_redispatched_agent_stops_reading_as_finished   the same invisibility
#                                                   one dispatch on: a stale
#                                                   terminal line no longer
#                                                   retires a re-dispatched agent
#   ..._a_dispatched_teammate_with_a_ledger_appears_exactly_once   the two halves
#                                                   of the roster key agents
#                                                   identically or one agent
#                                                   becomes two rows
# And the four guards against rebuilding D-022's silting, which pass both
# before and after the fix precisely because they pin what must NOT change:
#   ..._a_finished_wave_does_not_accumulate_on_the_roster   what retires a
#                                                   dispatch is the agent's own
#                                                   terminal line, not a clock
#   ..._a_terminal_line_written_after_the_dispatch_still_retires_it
#   ..._a_dispatch_younger_than_the_threshold_is_not_reported
#   ..._a_cast_dispatch_is_not_evidence_of_work_during_grind
#
# TEMPER cycle 6 added two sections, both about inputs that reached the
# arithmetic rather than the ledger. D-114 — a line dated in the FUTURE makes
# every age negative, a negative age is below every threshold, and the
# fall-through verdict was `progressing`, so a clock-skewed agent read healthy
# for as long as the skew lasted:
#   ..._a_future_dated_ledger_is_not_reported_as_progressing   the defect
#   ..._a_future_dated_agent_reaches_the_lead_as_a_call_to_action   its cost:
#                                                   needs_attention is the
#                                                   list the lead reads
#   ..._the_size_of_the_skew_cannot_buy_a_healthy_verdict   a year ahead is the
#                                                   same anomaly as an hour
#   ..._a_naive_local_timestamp_is_the_realistic_skew   not an adversarial
#                                                   input: the parser reads a
#                                                   naive stamp as UTC, so an
#                                                   agent east of Greenwich
#                                                   future-dates every line
#   ..._a_future_progress_line_is_caught_behind_a_recent_last_line   why BOTH
#                                                   ages are checked
#   ..._a_terminal_line_still_outranks_a_future_timestamp   the precedence the
#                                                   fix deliberately did NOT
#                                                   change, so D-022's silting
#                                                   is not rebuilt
# D-117 — `inf` and `nan` pass the positivity gate (`inf > 0` holds and every
# comparison against `nan` is False), make every agent read `progressing`, and
# then raise at `int(threshold)` across the MCP boundary:
#   ..._refuses_a_non_finite_threshold                     the defect
#   ..._infinity_is_reachable_over_the_wire_as_valid_json  1e400 is valid JSON
#   ..._negative_infinity_keeps_the_refusal_it_already_had  the path that was
#                                                   already correct is not
#                                                   re-routed by the fix
#   ..._a_non_finite_threshold_is_refused_before_any_agent_is_judged
# And D-115's adjacent path — the manifest reader this tool owns, which has
# always held the shape guard the two spawn doors were missing:
#   ..._a_wrong_typed_manifest_does_not_disturb_the_stream_roster
# GRIND cycle 19 (D-132) corrects a claim the paragraph above got wrong. This
# module's manifest reader — _skipped_stream_ids — did NOT hold the guard the
# spawn doors were missing; it held the same guard the doors held, at the same
# rung, with the same hole beneath it. `stream_skips: 42` reached
# `for entry in 42` and raised TypeError straight out of Foundry-Liveness, and
# `stream_skips: {"TRACE": "..."}` iterated the object's KEYS and silently
# STOPPED REPORTING the trace stream — a liveness tool hiding an agent because
# it misread its input. Both readers now consult one shared recursive validator
# (_manifest_shape_problem), so "unusable" means one thing in this module.
#
#   ..._a_wrong_typed_skip_list_degrades_instead_of_raising  three rows; the
#                                                   first two each failed
#                                                   before the fix, one by
#                                                   raising and one by silently
#                                                   dropping trace
#   ..._a_well_formed_skip_list_still_reaches_the_roster  the guard costs the
#                                                   working path nothing, which
#                                                   matters most here because a
#                                                   skip entry is legitimately
#                                                   EITHER an object or a bare
#                                                   string
#

